# Gemini Chatbot Implementation Plan

This plan outlines the architecture and steps to build a robust chatbot using the `google-genai` SDK, FastAPI for the backend, and Streamlit for the frontend.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> You will need a Google Gemini API Key. You can get one from [Google AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com/).
> We will store it in a `.env` file in the `backend/` directory.

## Proposed Changes

### Project Structure

We will adopt a modular structure to separate concerns:

```text
chat-bot/
├── backend/
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── main.py          # FastAPI application & endpoints
│   │   ├── agent.py         # Gemini AI logic (google-genai integration)
│   │   ├── schemas.py       # Pydantic models (request/response)
│   │   └── utils.py         # Helper functions for logging/formatting
│   ├── .env                 # Gemini API Key (keep this private)
│   └── requirements.txt     # Backend dependencies
├── frontend/
│   ├── app.py               # Streamlit UI
│   └── requirements.txt     # Frontend dependencies
└── .gitignore               # Ensure .env and venv are ignored
```

Phase 1: Environment & Dependencies Setup

    [#] Step 1.1: Populate backend/requirements.txt with your core web and AI libraries.
    [#] Step 1.2: Populate frontend/requirements.txt with your UI libraries.
    [#] Step 1.3: Create and activate a Python virtual environment (venv), then install both requirements files.

Phase 2: The Core AI Engine (backend/app/agent.py)

    [#] Step 2.1: Initialize the official Google GenAI client inside agent.py.
    [ ] Step 2.2: Write the asynchronous orchestration function (get_chat_response_stream) that formats raw conversational history into Gemini's native message structure and streams back token chunks.

Phase 3: Data Contracts & State Structure (backend/app/schemas.py)

    [ ] Step 3.1: Define the Pydantic data schemas for incoming client requests (ChatRequest) and individual message objects to ensure structural safety.

Phase 4: Server Architecture & Routing (backend/app/main.py)

    [ ] Step 4.1: Initialize your FastAPI application instance.
    [ ] Step 4.2: Configure Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CROS) middleware so your frontend server can securely talk to your backend server.
    [ ] Step 4.3: Set up an in-memory session dictionary (sessions = {}) to act as your temporary chat history database.
    [ ] Step 4.4: Write the POST endpoint /api/chat that intercepts the user's message, updates the history dictionary, triggers the streaming agent, and returns a real-time EventSource (Server-Sent Events) stream.

Phase 5: The User Interface (frontend/app.py)

    [ ] Step 5.1: Set up the Streamlit or Chainlit application page configuration.
    [ ] Step 5.2: Initialize Streamlit’s st.session_state to render past conversation bubbles on the UI.
    [ ] Step 5.3: Write the streaming client logic that posts a new message to the backend, captures the chunks via a streaming HTTP consumer, and updates the screen live.

Phase 6: System Launch & Verification

    [ ] Step 6.1: Spin up the Uvicorn backend server on localhost:8000.
    [ ] Step 6.2: Spin up the frontend UI server on localhost:8501.
    [ ] Step 6.3: Run an end-to-end multi-turn conversation test to confirm context retention.

